00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.180
So here's the threat vectors.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.830
We can see there are four
for this presentation.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:20.810
The first one is spam.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.804
Spam is just unsolicited
promotion messages.

00:01:23.804 --> 00:01:24.970
We've all got a lot of them.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.550
They are ubiquitous.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.790
They're not a threat, per se.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.182
But if they're
not kept in check,

00:01:30.182 --> 00:01:31.640
if the number is
not kept in check,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.420
then it becomes a significant
drain on the user's time

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.250
and resources and so on.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.680
I think recent
estimates put the damage

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.570
due to spam in only American
enterprises at about $20

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.610
billion per year.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.040
Then there's
phishing, of course.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.260
Phishing messages
impersonate legitimate email

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:52.550
communications.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.610
And they trick the user into
giving out their credentials

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.990
to the attacker, to the phisher.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.930
So most of them,
the one that you

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.340
might have also
gotten in your inbox,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.310
they are large-scale campaigns.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.539
So you would get an email says
that says, please click here

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.039
to reset your Paypal passwords.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:13.340
These are sent to
millions of users.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.170
But it's also important to talk
about spearphishing, especially

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.900
in the context of
the enterprise.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.510
These are targeted attacks that
use information about the user,

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.370
about the victim,
to be more effective

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.430
and also evade a lot
of defenses based

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:35.660
on trends and large
numbers because they

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.550
affect a single user.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.110
Then there is malware.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.670
So malware is just a
malicious payload that

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.620
is delivered by various means.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.530
They run with full privileges
on the user's machine.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.590
So they can do pretty
much whatever they want.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.730
Lately in the news
we've seen ransomware.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:54.910
It's been very popular.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:59.280
So this variant of malware
will encrypt your data

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.450
and then ask for a ransom
to decrypt your data

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.276
and get it back to you.

00:03:03.276 --> 00:03:04.650
But more traditionally
malware is

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.140
being used to get a
foothold into organizations.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.110
That's very common.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.310
So this is called advanced
persistent threat.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.670
Putting malware in one generic
user into the organization

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.850
is a good way of then expanding
your access in the organization

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.382
and staying undetected.

00:03:19.382 --> 00:03:21.880
And then there are
client attacks.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:23.840
So these are exploit
vulnerabilities

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.510
in clients and applications
and they execute

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.680
code on the victim's device.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.150
And depending on the
type of vulnerability,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.030
it could lead to the
same kind of infection

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.650
that you get from malware.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.160
Now Alberto is going to describe
some techniques that we employ

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.591
to mitigate these threats.

00:03:43.591 --> 00:03:44.840
ALBERTO ROJAS: Right, so yeah.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.360
We've enumerated a few
of these attack vectors.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.630
And well, we'll take a
quick tour of techniques

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:56.150
that we see these abuse actors
to deploy these attacks.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.830
And consequently,
we'll talk about what

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:01.760
we do to detect them,
predict them, and make sure

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.440
that we minimize the effect
that they have on our users.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.360
So he'll give you a glimpse
of what our team does,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.440
why it's a hard problem,
and also maybe it

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.370
will give you some insights
into what to do to prevent.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.200
There's a human
factor always present.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.320
And one uses to be aware and
never let their guard off.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.490
So we'll go through some
of these techniques, some

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:26.750
of these attack vectors.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.650
Like I said, there's too many
of them to cover in these talk.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.360
But we'll mention some of these.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.810
And hopefully we will give
you an idea of what we see

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.291
and what we do.

00:04:37.291 --> 00:04:37.790
All right.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.480
So first thing, I want to
imagine that there were no spam

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.170
filters.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.570
And there's someone
that's sending you

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.910
an email that you like.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.132
So you do like it
the first time.

00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:50.090
Don't think much of it.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.570
But then you keep
a counter, right?

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.074
You receive it for a second
time, maybe a third time.

00:04:55.074 --> 00:04:56.990
You notice that these
are all sent by someone,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.590
let's call them Bob.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.590
Well, what you might do,
if you have the ability

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:04.120
to write some rule,
would be to say

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.210
if the email's coming from
Bob, then let's mark it as red,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:11.360
delete it, put them
in some other folder.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.542
Well, that's more or less what
we do when people mark rules

00:05:16.542 --> 00:05:19.100
as spam or we find out
through other means

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.350
that an email is bad.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.470
We extract several
features from the email.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.630
And we keep account
on how often we

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.870
see bad emails versus
good emails showing those.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.000
You see here the screen
some examples of some

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.260
of those features.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.510
The obvious ones
are the IP address,

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:39.830
the email address of the
sender, and then more aggregated

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:41.870
features like, well,
class C networks,

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:45.950
or domains, tildes,
stats like the length

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:48.860
of the message, words
that are mentioned,

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.130
colors, aspect
ratios, you name it.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.590
And what we do is we
keep a counter keeping

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.920
a tally of how
often we see values

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:01.100
of those features
associated with badness.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.350
Now, if you want to
take it a step further,

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.910
you can combine this.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:09.260
Say an IP address is sort of
bad, you've seen a few of them.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:10.790
Maybe 20% are bad.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:13.550
It's hard to take and just put
them all in the spam folder.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.540
So what we do is combine them.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.120
Maybe you see partly
shady IP address,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.040
but the domain is really bad.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:25.720
Maybe those two combined
can be enough evidence

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.460
so that you can send an email,
actually, to the spam folder.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:33.080
Now, an even more
powerful technique

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.090
is if you can combine
those features

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:36.460
before you do the aggregation.

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:42.380
So now you're tracking how two
specific values of a feature

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.260
together are related to badness.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.150
So now we see what
value specifically

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.750
coming from one
ISP and containing

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.450
specific words, how
often those two together

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.150
are related to badness.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:58.520
Now, if you're doing the
math, imagine you have, like,

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.350
1,000 of these features.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:03.137
Pairwise combinations are
tuples of more than those.

00:07:03.137 --> 00:07:04.220
You could clearly explode.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.510
If you multiply that by
all the possible values,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.176
it's no way we could
keep track of that.

00:07:08.176 --> 00:07:09.960
So I guess part of
the secret sauce

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.710
that we have is figuring
out which of this to track

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:16.400
over time, how to evolve
them, and make sure

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.220
that we keep the set that
gives us the most return.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:28.870
Now if the person who's sending
you this email is persistent,

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:33.050
they would probably be
aware that you blocked them.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.420
And they will try to change
and go around your field.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.020
Nowadays, it's
easy enough to just

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:43.690
get a new IP address, certainly
getting a new email address.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:47.390
So what we want to
do is just make it

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:49.550
a little bit harder
for the abusers

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:50.860
to go around the protection.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.300
So repetition is
great but we need

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.620
to figure out what aspects
are hard for the abusers

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.050
to go around, and which
ones they can just

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.660
circumvent easily.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:06.410
So that prompts the question
of, what is it that--

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.810
for example, let's
focus on fishing--

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:10.730
what is inherent to fishing?

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:13.150
And it has to deceive someone.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:18.410
It has to prompt them into
clicking on this link,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.210
disclosing their
password information,

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.810
executing a file, and so on.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:24.139
So one way to give
legitimacy to that is

00:08:24.139 --> 00:08:25.430
to pretend to be someone else--

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:29.720
an authority, someone with
a lot of money, a business

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.659
partner of your organization,
depending on how much they

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:34.159
know about you.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:37.780
And we see, like the example
you see on the screen,

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:41.140
someone might be pretending
to be Google tech support.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:45.110
Now, if you just straight
out say Google tech support,

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:46.980
it's easy for us to catch that.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.540
So we'll try to do
something that's

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:54.080
called spoofing, which means you
write some sentence, some word,

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.880
that can be interpreted
as something,

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.420
but literally doesn't say that.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.830
So you can replace zeros for Os.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.115
You can use what's called
Unicode homoglyphs, which

00:09:05.115 --> 00:09:07.740
are characters that look exactly
the same that maybe characters

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.090
that you're used to seeing.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:12.190
But they're not
the same character.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:15.800
So any naive matching
algorithm would miss them.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.020
So of course, we need
to be aware of those

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:19.760
and check for similarity.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.130
We used canonicalization
of strings

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.420
to bring them back to a
form that can be compared.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.770
And there's a whole
spectrum of sophistication.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:31.250
So in the end of more
sophisticated attacks,

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:33.830
you can see even attacks
where the target a specific,

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:37.880
say, brand of smartphone,
where that phone makes

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:42.590
the two v's together look
like a w, but not in others.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:46.160
Even though that narrows
down the spectrum of victims

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.532
that can fall for this,
it also makes our job

00:09:49.532 --> 00:09:50.990
a little bit harder
because now you

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.340
have to pretend or try
to render these things

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:55.890
in different platforms.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:10:00.980
So you can see how this thing
is not that easy to scale.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:02.520
What they will
also do, of course,

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.650
is do the spoofing not only in
the display name that we'll use

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:10.790
sometimes, they will put an
email address in the display

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:16.020
name because you cannot easily
spoof the email address.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.660
But if you put it in
your display name,

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:20.270
pretending that it is your
email address, you could do it.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:22.020
Of course, we try
to detect that.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:28.000
You could also put strings
prompting you to click on this

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:29.910
or to inherit this
sum of money--

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:33.260
though we can also detect,
so they to spoof that.

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:35.700
Now, things get
more sophisticated.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.830
They move from
character-level spoofing

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:41.210
to images, which brings
us to the next topic.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:46.390
One way to also,
again, give legitimacy

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:52.030
to this deceitful
message is to provide

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:59.080
the same visual
similarity or visual theme

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.450
of a legitimate email.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.930
Now, you might just toss
a logo in there again.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.112
Pixel by pixel, it's pretty
easy for us to catch.

00:11:07.112 --> 00:11:09.320
But of course, it's also
easy for them to circumvent.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:14.470
You just randomly change in some
pixels, maybe color distances

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:17.200
that are hard for
the eye to catch.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:21.592
And then that force us to
make some more [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:21.592 --> 00:11:22.800
Then you can change the font.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.030
You can do stuff to
trick the filters.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:30.400
So we've been moving over
to machine intelligence--

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.280
so when we talk about
AI, we mention this--

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:36.580
to check whether a logo is
sufficiently similar to one

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:39.580
that we are trying to protect.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:43.210
And even the layout,
the fonts, the placement

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:46.900
of the bottoms,
the overall colors.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:51.100
Now just the fact
that something looks

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.882
like a legitimate one
from another brand

00:11:54.882 --> 00:11:56.590
doesn't mean the email
is bad, of course.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:00.610
So again, with the
feature repetition case

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:02.890
we combine these
things with others

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.890
so that we can relate
enough evidence

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.320
to decide whether an
email is good or bad.

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:15.510
Now about visual similarity,
we have to say this.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:20.660
We even see cases where there is
no additional visual indication

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:21.950
or advanced logos.

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.920
Just with the text, they're
still able to trick some user.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.400
If you're a domain
administrator,

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:32.000
nothing replaces a good
policy of keeping users aware

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.190
and constantly reminding
them to keep their guard up.

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:39.320
The last technique
we're going to cover

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:42.440
is about malicious attachments.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.180
And just like any anti-virus
check, we do checks on files.

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.690
Because files can be used,
as Ricardo mentioned,

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:53.450
to create a bot net
and have your computer

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:56.270
join someone else
to launch attacks

00:12:56.270 --> 00:13:00.512
against even other
organizations.

00:13:00.512 --> 00:13:02.720
They can install software
to watch what you're doing,

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:07.700
either to get your credentials
or just for espionage.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:09.380
And they can use ransomware.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:12.470
So there's a whole
spectrum of bad stuff

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.770
that can happen once they get a
foothold into an organization's

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:17.000
computer.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.790
Even if it's someone
not that consequential

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:22.350
in the organization
for financial purposes,

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.560
they can use that to
get to the person who

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:27.197
controls the accounts, to
the CEO, an information,

00:13:27.197 --> 00:13:28.530
do social engineering and so on.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.350
So we definitely
want to do all we

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:36.240
can to prevent these things from
ever being able to be executed.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:40.539
So Gmail outright disallows
some types of attachments

00:13:40.539 --> 00:13:41.330
from being present.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.690
But the spammers have
other ways to do this.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:49.130
They can take advantage of
traditionally non-executable

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:50.264
formats like documents.

00:13:50.264 --> 00:13:52.430
We use PDF here as an
example, but there are others.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.440
We can embed any binary object.

00:13:56.440 --> 00:13:58.190
There's also more shady
ways of embedding,

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.920
like executing macros
requiring the user

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:04.240
to interact with the document in
some way to launch its macros.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.260
It will even download
the content dynamically

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.320
so they can keep updating
this and changing it

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.140
so that the virus
signatures don't match.

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:14.510
So together with
advanced techniques

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:16.700
for extracting
these embeddings, we

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.390
need to apply techniques
for sandboxing

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:22.212
where we can
execute these things

00:14:22.212 --> 00:14:23.420
in a restrictive environment.

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:24.836
And we can monitor
whether they're

00:14:24.836 --> 00:14:27.620
trying to go home for a
command and control center

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:30.590
or trying to access your
files to encrypt them

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:33.060
in the case of
ransomware, and so on.

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:37.860
So Google has a range
of signatures that we

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.900
match against for regular AV.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:43.380
But we do all sorts of
prediction and these sandboxing

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:47.381
techniques to also apply to new
cases that we've never seen.

00:14:47.381 --> 00:14:48.880
We have the [INAUDIBLE]
which offers

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:53.570
configuration up [INAUDIBLE]
a domain administrator,

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:59.400
recommend you to keep in touch
with configuration options.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:01.740
Familiarize yourself
with them and determine

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:04.350
what their level of
comfort it is according

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:08.720
to the tier of the
product that you have

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.470
and the risk aversion,
versus how much you

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:14.340
want to delay message because
you want to scan them.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:19.200
We can [INAUDIBLE] to the inbox.

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:23.540
Now given the sheer
scale of email--

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.850
and frankly, the
sheer scale of spam,

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.910
which both range in
the billions per day,

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:31.610
and the time that this platform
or this medium of communication

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:33.380
has been around--

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:36.290
as you could probably
imagine, there's

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:39.980
a huge amount of
knowledge accumulated

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:42.620
over the years for
classification of spam

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.480
that would be very
hard for us to maintain

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:45.992
as a list of just rules.

00:15:45.992 --> 00:15:48.200
Like, getting rid of the
old ones that are not useful

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:52.080
anymore, merging ones that
have almost the same--

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:53.790
it would be a pretty
daunting task.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:16:00.060
So AI is a foundation that
lets us do several things.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:06.680
One of them is serve as a
repository of knowledge that

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:08.270
is constantly being updated.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:11.540
And things are not
relevant anymore get

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:15.807
taken out as part of
the training process.

00:16:15.807 --> 00:16:17.890
Artificial intelligence,
and machine learning most

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:21.620
in particular, also
adapt over time.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.140
New things, as long as
you see them in the field,

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.110
the systems will
learn to recognize it.

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:30.710
And thusly, depending on
the technique you use,

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:34.040
they also have the
capability to predict.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:37.080
So you show them examples
you've never seen before.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:39.190
They're supposed to
be used to catch them.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:40.190
We want to exploit that.

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:42.740
And that's why we
keep continually

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:48.950
trying new methods that will
allow us to be more proactive.

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:55.680
So in general, AI enables
Google to, well, protect users

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:56.280
against abuse.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.310
We also do recommendations
of all other cool stuff

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:01.000
with machine intelligence.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.170
But in the case
of Gmail, we were

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:09.290
one of the early adopters
within the organization

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:12.329
to harness the power of AI.

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:15.170
But over the years,
we've built up

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:19.859
like a pretty good mix of
traditional proven techniques.

00:17:19.859 --> 00:17:25.520
And we're sort of cutting
edge newer technologies.

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.920
We have a pretty significant
linear regression classifier

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:31.230
model because that's
what we started with

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:32.830
and we still keep it.

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:34.865
But over the time,
what we've come up with

00:17:34.865 --> 00:17:39.650
is a multilayer,
multipipeline set up.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:45.870
And by multilayer I mean take
a [INAUDIBLE] neural network

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:50.220
that use to recognize visual
features in something-- a logo,

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:56.400
or the presence of some
particular graphic element.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:59.471
That in itself usually won't
be enough to classify something

00:17:59.471 --> 00:17:59.970
as bad.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:03.390
So again, one more feature
that we can extract,

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.835
VM machine learning to
determine, yes, there

00:18:05.835 --> 00:18:06.460
there's a logo.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:07.500
There's not a logo.

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.720
Then you combine that
with other features.

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:14.370
And you can feed that
into either manual rules

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:16.440
or, more likely,
to another model.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.450
So we have multiple layers.

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:23.400
And we have parallel
pipelines in such a way

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.840
that even the final verdict we
can get from multiple master

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:30.570
models so we can put
them against each other,

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:34.740
make sure that we're getting
the best of both worlds.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:38.280
You can get a second opinion
of sorts, if you will.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:40.680
So the machine
intelligence landscape

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:44.440
is one that you have to keep
continually maintaining.

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:47.100
Now, it would be great if
this would maintain itself.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:48.780
It's not so.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:53.010
So our team needs to
keep continually adapting

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:56.040
the training of the models,
monitoring how well it's

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:58.020
detecting, what it's
supposed to be detecting,

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.120
and checking
against new attacks.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.340
So some challenges that
we find is, well, it

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:05.270
starts with the training.

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:12.174
You sample specific instances
of both malicious emails

00:19:12.174 --> 00:19:12.840
and good emails.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.060
You need to teach the
system to recognize

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:17.850
both with the right
set of features.

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:21.140
You need to make sure that
they are labeled correctly.

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:25.030
You cannot take every
spam mark as face value.

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:27.510
You have to do some sort
of clean up and make sure

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:30.820
that those run reliably
and constantly.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:33.780
You are able to train your
models with enough cadence--

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.630
be that every month
or week, every day,

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:38.520
or maybe continuous
learning models.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:40.920
You have to get
into that cadence

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.320
to make sure that you're
constantly discarding

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.240
all information,
incorporate a new one,

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:48.810
keeping your model
tight and optimized.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:50.610
Another aspect that
is particular to spam

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:53.340
is that not everyone
agrees what is spam.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.260
What for some person
might be completely

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.210
undesirable for
some other might be

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:00.180
a mild annoyance, for some
other person might be just,

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:02.260
like, oh, useful
advertisement that they happen

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:03.510
to read every once in a while.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:09.710
So it will be pretty hard for
us to keep a personalized, fully

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:14.390
fledged machine learning model
for every one of our users.

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:17.340
After all, these models
do take up space.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.390
But luckily, you
don't have to do that.

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:22.940
There's a big section of
the email spectrum where

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:25.980
you can pretty much agree that
they're good, that they're bad.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:30.650
We can keep the gray area
as a parametrized option

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:32.990
where you can feed it
with other signals.

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:34.970
You can tell your
model, OK, these

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:37.010
are the features coming
in from the email.

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:40.800
But these are also the
preferences of the user.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:42.590
So you can adjust
your thresholds

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:46.180
and make the best decision
for each of our users.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:49.310
Now, another challenge is
models will make mistakes.

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:52.562
You need to have a strategy
to deal with those mistakes,

00:20:52.562 --> 00:20:54.020
to treat them
differently depending

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:58.640
on the type of mistake there
is, and to systematically

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:02.840
diminish them over time.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:05.540
It is true that models,
like I said before, they

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:07.580
will adapt over time.

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:10.650
And they will predict
new situations.

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:16.080
But when they fail, typically
they don't recover instantly.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:18.020
It will take time
for them to catch up.

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:23.831
That means we do have a rules
engine, blacklist, other maybe

00:21:23.831 --> 00:21:25.080
less sophisticated techniques.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:29.790
But you need those to
account for those gaps.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:31.650
Now, we talked about errors.

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.660
Errors are not the same.

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:36.060
There's different
types of errors.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.320
And there's different
problem spaces.

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:39.700
Take spam, for example.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:47.220
If we miss a a spam email,
ends up in your inbox,

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:48.000
it's an annoyance.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:51.630
You might not think that much
of it, and maybe delete it.

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:54.020
Hopefully you'll mark it
as spam and help us learn.

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:55.960
But maybe that's the end of it.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:00.870
But on the other hand,
if we take a good email

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:02.712
and put it in the
spam folder, that

00:22:02.712 --> 00:22:06.019
might make you miss something
important for your business.

00:22:06.019 --> 00:22:08.310
It might make you miss
something important from someone

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:09.434
you know, and so on and so.

00:22:09.434 --> 00:22:11.820
Clearly they're not the same.

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:14.550
For phishing, it's kind of
the opposite, but not quite.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:18.850
Say if we send something
to the spam folder,

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:20.950
we think it was phishing
ans you miss it, yeah.

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:22.800
We already established
that is bad.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:26.310
But if we really miss a phishing
email, put it in your inbox,

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:31.190
you execute that, the potential
for disaster is much bigger.

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:34.840
So there's really no good
or bad error in here.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:36.820
There's still imbalance,
but both are bad.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:38.990
So what we do in
that case is we try

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:42.070
to mitigate the effects of
these errors by having what

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:43.240
we can call softer hammers.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:45.010
Like in the case
you're not completely

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:47.560
sure something is a phishing
email, instead of sending it

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:50.500
to the spam folder, we
can put some warnings

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:53.110
in place, some UI hints.

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:56.440
And Riccardo will
cover some of those

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:58.960
so that if you're a
domain administrator

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:01.810
you can keep your
users aware and always

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:03.510
on the lookout
for these signals.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:10.210
How this all looks together--
like I said, over the years,

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:14.110
we have a pretty significant
linear classifier.

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:19.120
So of the 99.9% percent of
spammy and harmful email

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:22.420
that we detect,
about 9/10 of that

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:26.470
is already caught
by this classifier.

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:35.520
But of the remaining percentage,
but have a various heuristics,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:39.790
that means this rule engine
that I was mentioning earlier

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:43.780
and the rest is with more
advanced machine learning

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:44.830
techniques.

00:23:44.830 --> 00:23:46.330
Now, this is not
the full picture.

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:49.730
This is not, like,
disjoint sets.

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:50.997
There's a lot of overlap.

00:23:50.997 --> 00:23:52.330
We try to maximize that overlap.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:54.090
Actually, that's by design.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.480
This is a concept
called defense in depth.

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:58.270
If you want to get
through our defenses,

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:01.350
you should always need to
break more than one thing so

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:03.140
that it's not easy
for you that you just

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:07.380
hit the jackpot and one
thing and then you're in.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:10.090
So what we see here is
only additional coverage.

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:12.640
And we would like to
keep that small so

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:15.210
that there's no single
point where you can just

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.505
trick our defenses and get in.

00:24:17.505 --> 00:24:20.270
That question mark you see there
is, of course, next generation.

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:22.700
We're always investigating
new techniques

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:28.180
that might be high or low
tech, that might involve

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:29.560
machine intelligence or not.

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:32.560
We're constantly
deriving new features,

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:33.940
pushing for new
standards that we

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:38.740
can use to protect the identity
or the integrity of emails,

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:39.320
and so on.

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:42.160
So that's a constantly
evolving field.

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:45.910
So next we'll go to see how
we perform the classification

00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:48.910
and what it ends up
meaning to our users

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:51.760
and how can identify
what happened

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:53.180
within the
classification process.

00:24:55.975 --> 00:24:56.850
RICCARDO PELIZZI: OK.

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:59.540
So Alberto's shown all
these powerful techniques--

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:02.510
AV scanning, how the
spam classifier works.

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:04.100
So how does Gmail
put it all together

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:06.141
to protect the users
against these threat vectors

00:25:06.141 --> 00:25:08.120
that we presented before?

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:11.750
To explain that I think
it's easy to just go

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:14.390
through the flow of a
message from the sender

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:16.580
to the user, the end user.

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:18.320
And we're not talking
just about going

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:21.260
from the outbox of the sender
to the inbox of the user.

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:24.140
But we'll also consider how the
user interacts with the message

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:27.570
once it has been
delivered to the inbox.

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:28.070
OK.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:29.869
So in particular, we
are interested in what

00:25:29.869 --> 00:25:31.910
happens when the message
is malicious, of course,

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:34.550
because if our purpose is
to showcase the defenses,

00:25:34.550 --> 00:25:36.830
hopefully nothing happens
if the message is benign.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:40.760
And so we are going to see how
a malicious message flows--

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:42.530
you know, we're going
to see the lifecycle

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:44.830
of a malicious message.

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:48.464
And here are all the relevant
steps in more detail.

00:25:48.464 --> 00:25:49.880
They are sorted
in temporal order.

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:51.340
So on the left,
you see delivery,

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:52.340
which is the first step.

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:55.900
And then all the
way to the right,

00:25:55.900 --> 00:25:59.890
you see actions that are
taken by the user on the email

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:02.440
because you're soon
going from left to right.

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:05.366
Your eyes already opened
it and everything.

00:26:05.366 --> 00:26:07.240
We are willing to go
through each one of them

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:09.920
in detail, of course, because
there are many [INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:10.420
OK.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:12.730
So note that something
is not shown in this flow

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:15.700
is that delivery and sync,
the first two phases,

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:18.010
happen on the server.

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:23.110
So they will be able to use all
the intelligence that Alberto

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:24.430
talked about.

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:29.350
All the other phases
happen on the client.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:32.720
Also note how re-classification
is not quite defined

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:35.405
in a single place in the order.

00:26:35.405 --> 00:26:37.780
That's because, by definition,
it can happen at any time.

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:43.550
Re-classification is the process
of rescanning old messages

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:46.870
with updated rules, updated
AV signatures and so on,

00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:51.790
and making changing the
verdict of the classifier

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:53.540
and syncing the
information to the client.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:57.200
So this can happen at
anytime, or not happen at all.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:59.020
You can start this
off into any moment.

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:01.880
OK.

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:06.050
So here all the defenses that we
provided during the lifecycle.

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:08.300
So Alberto mentioned before
about the spam classifier,

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:09.740
the defense in depth.

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:11.940
This is another example
of defense in depth.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:14.920
So as you can see, through flow
from all the way to the left

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:16.550
to all the way to
the right, you have

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:19.010
to go through a lot
of these defenses.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:21.980
So there's multiple
layers here, too.

00:27:21.980 --> 00:27:25.640
If you are overwhelmed with the
amount of red crosses and names

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.860
on the slide, that's
perfectly fine.

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:29.180
That actually was intended.

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.760
This slide is meant
to be overwhelming

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:34.160
because it gives me an
excuse to now go through

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:36.830
all the various
phases and explain

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:40.200
how all these defenses
affect the delivery

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:46.590
and an ongoing attack when
it comes to malicious email.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.200
So the first phase is delivery.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:53.340
This happens when we receive
the message in our main servers

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:55.320
but we haven't delivered
it to the user yet.

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:58.390
And in fact, we might
not deliver it at all.

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:01.170
So a lot of the spam messages--
or spam phishing, malware,

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:02.460
or whatever--

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:05.580
never makes the user's inbox
or even the spam folder.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.410
So we don't just
decide whether we

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:09.330
send an email to the
inbox or the spam folder.

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:11.289
We can also reject
it altogether.

00:28:11.289 --> 00:28:13.830
If you think you have a lot of
spam email in your spam folder

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:17.050
right now, imagine if
you had 10 times as much.

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:19.890
That's more or less, I
think, an approximate figure

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:22.110
about how much we
reject altogether

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.860
versus what we put in spam.

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.430
Note that most of our detection
capabilities are here.

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:29.430
As I mentioned before,
this is one of the phases

00:28:29.430 --> 00:28:30.513
that happen on the server.

00:28:30.513 --> 00:28:33.810
So this is where all of our
intelligence are available.

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.920
The later phases,
we basically use

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:39.695
metadata that has been generated
here and use it because now

00:28:39.695 --> 00:28:41.070
when you're on
the client now you

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:44.840
can have user visible
effects besides rejecting.

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:47.235
The most common bit of
metadata, to give an example,

00:28:47.235 --> 00:28:49.260
is, of course, the spam bit.

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:51.580
Is it spam or is it non-spam.

00:28:51.580 --> 00:28:54.030
So does it go in the inbox,
does it go in the spam folder?

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:55.654
But there are other
pieces of meta-data

00:28:55.654 --> 00:28:58.650
that we're going to show.

00:28:58.650 --> 00:29:01.402
So the second phase is
sync, so again, we're

00:29:01.402 --> 00:29:03.860
going to skip re-classification
because it doesn't actually

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:05.900
have an order.

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:07.280
The second phase is sync.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:12.090
So this is when the servers
push the message to the client

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:14.130
so that then becomes
available to the user

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:18.680
and sees it in the thread listed
in the list of unread messages.

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:22.960
From a security standpoint, we
need to do to two main tasks

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:23.460
here.

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:27.210
So the first one is to propagate
all this security relevant

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.230
metadata that I
mentioned before.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:32.090
So that will became my
client-side defenses.

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:35.840
The second main goal is to
sanitize untrusted content.

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:39.050
The main purpose here is
to sanitize the message

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:42.950
body, which of course, the
email protocol doesn't define

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:46.280
anything related to what
kind of malicious content

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:49.370
can appear in the body, how
much the HTML can be dynamic,

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:50.414
and so on.

00:29:50.414 --> 00:29:51.830
So we do have a
very strict policy

00:29:51.830 --> 00:29:54.529
about what can be served
on the user's device.

00:29:54.529 --> 00:29:56.820
The idea is that the server
does the heavy lifting when

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:58.110
it comes to sanitization.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:29:59.927
So the client gets
a piece of HTML

00:29:59.927 --> 00:30:01.260
that is already safe to display.

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:05.220
And then we have message open.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.782
So this is the phase that
happens when the user--

00:30:08.782 --> 00:30:10.740
assuming the user selects
a message-- opens it.

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:12.790
And now you can
see its contents.

00:30:12.790 --> 00:30:16.740
So this is a good place
for us to give visual cues

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:19.470
about security issues so we can
inform the user about something

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:22.650
that is maybe relevant, or warn
the user about something that

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:24.810
is definitely suspicious.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.690
All these cues are
about those gray areas

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:28.530
that Alberto talked about.

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.240
If an email is
definitely malicious,

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:31.440
then it wouldn't get here.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.450
But there's a long
trail of e-mails

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:35.620
that have some
suspicious behavior

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:39.030
or features that we can't
quite put in the spam folder.

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:42.750
So this will be where we start
warning the user from now on.

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:46.800
One example is, of course,
showing a warning button,

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:52.050
so that a banner that says,
look, this could be phishing.

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:54.180
These words were often
associated with phishing,

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:55.690
so be careful.

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:57.210
Another example of
a visual cue here

00:30:57.210 --> 00:31:00.820
is an up-to-date virus check.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:05.190
So we issue a virus
check, the same thing that

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:06.690
has been done during delivery.

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:09.120
We redo it when the
message is opened.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:11.940
And the reason why we redo it
is because potentially the AV

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:14.950
has been updated with
signatures and the AV

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:17.610
could give a different verdict.

00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:19.670
[INAUDIBLE] you're supposed
to take care of that.

00:31:19.670 --> 00:31:21.637
So we do have eventual
consistency, eventually

00:31:21.637 --> 00:31:23.970
the classification will do
it even when the user doesn't

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:25.525
open and push the update.

00:31:25.525 --> 00:31:27.150
But the issue is that
re-classification

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:31.620
is a background job that
could take minutes to execute.

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:34.290
And the idea is we want to close
this window of vulnerability.

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:38.960
As soon as the AV
has a new signature,

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:41.786
it should have a visible
effect on the user's inbox.

00:31:41.786 --> 00:31:43.410
So in that case, we
would warn the user

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:44.368
and prevent a download.

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:48.540
After the message
is open, the user

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:49.780
is going to interact with it.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:51.300
And depending on
what type of threat

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.530
we talk about, if it's
phishing, malware, and so on,

00:31:53.530 --> 00:31:55.650
we focus on different actions.

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:59.270
If your message is
carrying an exploit,

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:01.770
then the most relevant phase
is the download and attachment.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:04.440
So let's go through a
scenario where the user sends

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:08.410
a malicious PDF that is supposed
to exploit an Adobe Reader

00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:10.710
vulnerability.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:14.190
So the first phase is that
maybe nothing happens at all

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.110
because, as you said
before, during delivery

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:18.040
we could reject the message.

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:22.360
So the user might
not see that at all.

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:25.720
The inbox is still empty
because the email was never

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:27.662
delivered to the user.

00:32:27.662 --> 00:32:29.370
So let's assume, again,
defense in depth.

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:31.460
So let's assume that
one-layer phase.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:34.570
The message gets through
because the signatures

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:36.710
were not up to date.

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.790
This is a very novel threat
that are AV's couldn't handle.

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:43.580
So the user then sees the
message and decides to open it.

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:45.150
So all is not lost.

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:48.790
So we have a few extra,
additional layers of defense.

00:32:48.790 --> 00:32:50.951
For example, we have
not just a virus scan

00:32:50.951 --> 00:32:52.450
but we also have a
lot of heuristics

00:32:52.450 --> 00:32:53.840
regarding what is risky.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.930
So if the attachment
is suspicious,

00:32:55.930 --> 00:32:57.640
then we can at
least warn the user.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:32:59.848
We don't have enough confidence
to reject the message

00:32:59.848 --> 00:33:02.200
because we haven't matched
an exact virus simple,

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:05.470
but we can at least tell the
user that the attachment is

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:06.970
suspicious.

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:10.060
And for that, we can
use the warning banners.

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:19.972
As I mentioned before, even
if the attachment was not

00:33:19.972 --> 00:33:22.180
malicious-- or at least was
not detected as malicious

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:25.000
by the virus when the
message was delivered--

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:26.440
it's possible that
it's because it

00:33:26.440 --> 00:33:29.290
was due to an out of the virus
signature that eventually gets

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:32.020
updated because of course
we continually improves

00:33:32.020 --> 00:33:32.780
our signatures.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:36.880
And we also purchase updates
from anti-virus vendors.

00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:38.782
So it's possible
that at some point

00:33:38.782 --> 00:33:40.240
the antivirus will
change its mind.

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:45.070
So as I said before, we do
an AV check message open.

00:33:45.070 --> 00:33:47.430
And that would prevent
you from opening the file.

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:51.610
As I said, the
reason is, of course,

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:53.650
because the re-classification
can take time.

00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:56.767
And we want to reduce this
window of vulnerability.

00:33:56.767 --> 00:33:58.600
So let's assume that
the exploit is also not

00:33:58.600 --> 00:34:00.610
known when the user
opens the message.

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:04.570
So even the up-to-date virus
check doesn't detect anything.

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:09.880
So the user is not alerted
and falls into the trap

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:11.790
and opens the attachment.

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.284
Well, the user is still not
going to be compromised.

00:34:14.284 --> 00:34:16.659
The reason for this is that
for a lot of very common file

00:34:16.659 --> 00:34:19.129
types like PDF and
doc and so on, we

00:34:19.129 --> 00:34:20.170
have an embedded preview.

00:34:20.170 --> 00:34:24.520
This is just an HTML
preview of the content that

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.179
appears in the browser
window so that, of course,

00:34:27.179 --> 00:34:31.730
it's not vulnerable
to PDF exploits.

00:34:31.730 --> 00:34:34.252
So if you get
exploited finally--

00:34:34.252 --> 00:34:35.710
which unfortunately
can happen-- is

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:41.030
that the user needs to also
download the attachment.

00:34:41.030 --> 00:34:42.530
So let's go through
a different path

00:34:42.530 --> 00:34:46.250
now than is usually taken
by a phishing email instead.

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:48.560
So in a phishing
email, you're probably

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:52.055
going to have some text that
entices the user into clicking

00:34:52.055 --> 00:34:55.429
on a link, going on a
website and entering

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:58.730
their password because you
ask for the credentials.

00:34:58.730 --> 00:35:01.610
This is probably going to be
looking like a common service

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:02.690
like Google.

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:05.300
And it will ask
for the passwords.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:06.890
So let's go over
the scenario again.

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:08.810
So first thing is
that we are going

00:35:08.810 --> 00:35:11.554
to assume that the previous
defenses have already failed.

00:35:11.554 --> 00:35:13.970
Not because they are more
likely to fail for phishing-- so

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:15.830
we're talking about
rejecting a delivery

00:35:15.830 --> 00:35:19.022
and showing all the various
message [INAUDIBLE]..

00:35:19.022 --> 00:35:20.480
Not because you're
more likely to--

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:21.688
but just because for privacy.

00:35:21.688 --> 00:35:23.160
We have defense in that.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:24.320
We know how those work.

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:27.470
So let's move on to the phase
that is different for now.

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:30.340
So let's assume that the
message gets to the user inbox.

00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:32.590
The user opens it and that
there is no warning related

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:34.340
to phishing through banners.

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:37.470
And therefore it clicks on
the link, like in this case.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:40.922
Please click here to
reset your password.

00:35:40.922 --> 00:35:42.380
Well, there are
two things that can

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:44.338
happen that will prevent
the user from actually

00:35:44.338 --> 00:35:45.900
going to the site.

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:49.570
The first is that in those
gray areas where the back end

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:56.320
classifier has low confidence
about the reputation

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:59.720
of the link, we can at
least show a warning prompt.

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:01.850
This will hopefully
discourage the user

00:36:01.850 --> 00:36:04.510
about proceeding to
the site, or at least

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:07.610
alert him that he needs to be
extra careful on this site.

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:09.370
And the second
thing is that we are

00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:11.780
going to do an up-to-date
safe browsing check.

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:14.560
Now, the browser also has a--

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:16.970
Chrome, at least-- has a
safe browsing blacklist

00:36:16.970 --> 00:36:20.140
synced on the client.

00:36:20.140 --> 00:36:22.430
But the one using the
[INAUDIBLE] is more up to date

00:36:22.430 --> 00:36:23.630
and more comprehensive.

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:26.190
The point is, again,
being more up to date

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:28.940
than the classification,
which also would detect this,

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:31.520
but that can take time.

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:33.230
And even if the
user gets no warning

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:35.780
and enters the
password, if the user

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:37.910
is taking proactive
steps then even

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:40.220
then the attacker account
is now compromised.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.230
So for example, two
step verification

00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:44.090
means that stealing
the password is not

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:45.770
enough to access the account.

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:48.110
We had a webinar, I
think, a month ago

00:36:48.110 --> 00:36:51.340
regarding how to set it
up for your organization.

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:52.220
And-- oh, sorry.

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:54.440
And the other thing that we
also discussed last month

00:36:54.440 --> 00:36:56.810
was password reset
extension, which

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:59.481
also needs to be set up for the
organization or for the user.

00:36:59.481 --> 00:37:00.980
And it's very useful
because it will

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:05.590
reset the password immediately
if it's typed on another site.

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:07.670
Another type of phishing
attack is oAuth threat.

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:09.211
So this is the idea
that we are going

00:37:09.211 --> 00:37:12.160
to phish for oAuth permissions
instead of credentials.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:16.050
And it's effective
because users are

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:20.190
less wary of delegating access
or granting permission compared

00:37:20.190 --> 00:37:22.560
to giving up credentials.

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:25.620
At least for enterprises,
we can block these attacks

00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:28.110
by whitelisting which
applications can

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:30.042
ask for permission to the user.

00:37:30.042 --> 00:37:32.250
And then the final scenario
that I want to go through

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:34.670
is business email compromise.

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:36.420
So this is very important
for enterprises.

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:39.750
And the idea of this attack
is that the phisher entices

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.839
the user to send confidential
information directly

00:37:42.839 --> 00:37:44.630
to the attacker by
replying to the message,

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:46.710
so not by going
through our site,

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:48.330
but just by replying
to the email.

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:49.955
And let's go through
a scenario and see

00:37:49.955 --> 00:37:52.530
how our defenses play with it.

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:54.970
So let's assume that the user
gets an email from a CEO.

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:58.800
This is an employee who receives
an email from the CEO and says,

00:37:58.800 --> 00:37:59.697
please, it's urgent.

00:37:59.697 --> 00:38:00.780
Please give us the detail.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:02.109
Can you send them to us?

00:38:02.109 --> 00:38:03.900
And so first thing is
that the user are not

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:06.000
so easy to fool
when they use Gmail

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.340
because most of
these attacks require

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:12.106
the sender to pretend to be
in the user's organization.

00:38:12.106 --> 00:38:14.230
So one defense that is
super effective against this

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:15.300
is out of domain warning.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:16.966
We warn the user that
this person is not

00:38:16.966 --> 00:38:17.799
in the organization.

00:38:17.799 --> 00:38:19.440
So obviously the
user will immediately

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:21.501
figure out that something
fishy is happening.

00:38:21.501 --> 00:38:23.250
But let's assume,
again, defense in depth.

00:38:23.250 --> 00:38:25.050
Let's assume that this fails.

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:30.132
So the user gets fooled
and he presses send.

00:38:30.132 --> 00:38:31.590
The data is still
not going to be--

00:38:31.590 --> 00:38:33.270
if the admin has
taken proactive steps,

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:36.060
the data is not going
to be leaked anyway.

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:38.680
We have this feature called
data loss prevention that

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:41.130
will run on outgoing
email and scan it

00:38:41.130 --> 00:38:43.810
for sensitive information
such as social security number

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:44.800
and bank accounts.

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:47.970
So in this case, the
user is typing something.

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:52.570
And this is an IBAN, a European
Banking Account Identifier.

00:38:52.570 --> 00:38:54.810
And if we have proper
VIP rules in place,

00:38:54.810 --> 00:38:59.320
we can stop this message from
actually going to the phisher.

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:01.906
So to recap, we went
through these defenses.

00:39:01.906 --> 00:39:04.030
Hopefully now that you've
seen them all one by one,

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:06.810
this slide is less daunting.

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:08.850
The main takeaway is,
again, defense in depth.

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:13.240
So many layers before
a user can be exploited

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:16.530
and can be attached.

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:18.420
And that's it for the talk.

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:20.700
So we are going to
be moving on to Q&amp;A

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:23.100
now after a very short break,
I think, around a minute.

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:31.762
ALBERTO ROJAS:
OK, so we're back.

00:40:31.762 --> 00:40:32.970
We have a few questions here.

00:40:32.970 --> 00:40:38.100
And remember that if we didn't
go through your questions,

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:41.100
we'll go through them
offline on the platform.

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:43.270
So the first one is
what should users

00:40:43.270 --> 00:40:45.940
be aware of regarding new
trends in phishing and malware?

00:40:45.940 --> 00:40:53.250
Well, there's a growing trend
of going to actual malware.

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:59.010
As you get better at
detecting phishing attacks,

00:40:59.010 --> 00:41:01.780
there's this ongoing
trend that we're

00:41:01.780 --> 00:41:04.760
seeing of moving on
to malware attacks.

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:06.600
They're easier to hide.

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:12.880
It's hard for us to execute
every single malicious content

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:15.604
and attack every single
shady thing that it will do.

00:41:15.604 --> 00:41:17.020
Like imagine
something that allows

00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:20.230
you to click on some
elements of the UI.

00:41:20.230 --> 00:41:23.380
We can only do so
much about that.

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:27.600
So it's a growing
threat, our detection

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:29.950
of content being
downloaded into the user's

00:41:29.950 --> 00:41:32.260
computer and the
ability to execute

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:34.930
that in a safe
environment for us

00:41:34.930 --> 00:41:37.240
to determine whether
it's harmful.

00:41:37.240 --> 00:41:40.330
It's something we're
investing heavily on.

00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:46.020
There's also, within
the fishing arena it's

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:48.300
moving on to more spearfishing.

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:51.220
Like, they will invest in
knowing a little bit more

00:41:51.220 --> 00:41:54.160
about the target and
will try to convince them

00:41:54.160 --> 00:41:56.560
with specific information.

00:41:56.560 --> 00:41:59.200
Viral phish has been going down.

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:02.890
But the spearfishing--
it's less in numbers.

00:42:02.890 --> 00:42:05.110
It's less prevalent in numbers.

00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:07.720
But they're much more
damaging and much

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:09.970
more likely to succeed.

00:42:09.970 --> 00:42:11.770
Like we've repeated
during this talk,

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:15.230
nothing replaces a
good awareness program.

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:17.490
And be constantly reminded
of this for the users

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:20.135
to never let their guard off.

00:42:20.135 --> 00:42:22.510
So similar question, what are
specifically the best steps

00:42:22.510 --> 00:42:24.370
to protect against
spear phishing?

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:27.380
Well, some of the things we
covered in the previous talk.

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:30.620
Strongly suggest you go
ahead and watch that.

00:42:30.620 --> 00:42:34.600
But heed the warnings.

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:38.550
We try our best not make any of
our warnings a sort of cry wolf

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:42.850
so that they become
unconsciously bypassed

00:42:42.850 --> 00:42:44.070
by a user.

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:48.010
So here we also need
the collaboration

00:42:48.010 --> 00:42:49.990
of the main administrators
to make sure

00:42:49.990 --> 00:42:52.720
that people heed those warnings.

00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:54.670
Data loss prevention--
you need to configure

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:57.130
it to work correctly according
to the types of information

00:42:57.130 --> 00:43:04.270
you want to prevent from getting
out of your organization.

00:43:04.270 --> 00:43:06.760
We'll look at your
domain directory.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:09.430
People are spoofing
the name of the CEO.

00:43:09.430 --> 00:43:11.170
Keep those updated.

00:43:11.170 --> 00:43:14.110
There's many things you can
do to improve the security

00:43:14.110 --> 00:43:16.455
of your whole enterprise.

00:43:16.455 --> 00:43:17.830
RICCARDO PELIZZI:
For this one, I

00:43:17.830 --> 00:43:19.550
think the most
important one, even

00:43:19.550 --> 00:43:23.420
though it takes some effort, is
set up two-step verification.

00:43:23.420 --> 00:43:26.270
If you really care about
not getting spearfished,

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:29.500
definitely buy a
U2F security key.

00:43:29.500 --> 00:43:31.580
They cost, what, $20?

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:32.180
You set it up.

00:43:32.180 --> 00:43:33.110
Google supports it.

00:43:33.110 --> 00:43:37.490
And that is, I think, the best
way of preventing spearfishing,

00:43:37.490 --> 00:43:40.610
or at least preventing
the attacker

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:44.790
from accessing your account
when your password gets phished.

00:43:44.790 --> 00:43:47.450
And if you're an enterprise,
then, the best thing to do

00:43:47.450 --> 00:43:49.830
is buying all your
employees this key.

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:50.330
OK.

00:43:50.330 --> 00:43:53.090
So the third question
is for mobile clients.

00:43:53.090 --> 00:43:56.352
Are there protections
available there, as well?

00:43:56.352 --> 00:43:57.810
Are all these
protection available?

00:43:57.810 --> 00:43:59.377
Yes, most of them are.

00:43:59.377 --> 00:44:00.960
So sometimes the
challenge, of course,

00:44:00.960 --> 00:44:04.766
is how do you show these
cues in a reduced UI?

00:44:04.766 --> 00:44:06.890
But most of-- for example,
the banners that we saw,

00:44:06.890 --> 00:44:08.150
they are all available.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:11.020
And I think all
the stuff that is

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:13.697
based on server side
functionality like DLP

00:44:13.697 --> 00:44:14.780
or rejecting on delivery--

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:15.730
ALBERTO ROJAS: Absolutely.

00:44:15.730 --> 00:44:16.850
RICCARDO PELIZZI: And
putting in the spam folder,

00:44:16.850 --> 00:44:18.200
that's also all available.

00:44:18.200 --> 00:44:20.600
I think of the ones that
we saw, the only two

00:44:20.600 --> 00:44:22.880
that were not available were--

00:44:22.880 --> 00:44:26.820
one was anti-virus
checking up to date.

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:30.440
But of course, on mobile viruses
delivered through attachments

00:44:30.440 --> 00:44:33.210
are not as big of a threat
because you cannot just execute

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:35.850
an APK on Android, for example.

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:38.510
And most malware ends
up being for Windows.

00:44:38.510 --> 00:44:41.360
And the other one was
preview because we

00:44:41.360 --> 00:44:45.090
use the embedded previewer
on your mobile device.

00:44:45.090 --> 00:44:46.880
For example, on Android we use--

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:48.870
I forget what it's called.

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:49.470
PDF reader.

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:52.200
But anyway, it's, of
course, on Android

00:44:52.200 --> 00:44:54.547
the applications are
usually written in Java,

00:44:54.547 --> 00:44:56.630
or at least some of the
parts are written in Java.

00:44:56.630 --> 00:45:01.520
And they are more
resilient from exploits.

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:02.660
That's all, right?

00:45:02.660 --> 00:45:03.790
OK, yeah.

00:45:03.790 --> 00:45:04.960
That's all the questions.

00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:07.190
So please tune in next Tuesday.

00:45:07.190 --> 00:45:09.710
We're going to
have more webinars.

00:45:09.710 --> 00:45:11.180
So I have the topics here.

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:13.915
So we have data analytics
and machine learning,

00:45:13.915 --> 00:45:17.590
IT infrastructure, and
collaboration and productivity.

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:21.250
So thank you for watching
and see you guys next time.

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:22.800
ALBERTO ROJAS: Thanks.

